  "summary": "Sir John Dodson (1780–1858) was an English judge, church lawyer and Tory MP. He represented Rye in Parliament from 1819 to 1823, then held senior legal posts in the Admiralty and church courts.\n\nAppointed Admiralty advocate in 1829, he became King’s Advocate in 1834 and was knighted that year. He served as judge of the Prerogative Court of Canterbury from 1852 until its jurisdiction was abolished in 1857, and as Dean of the Arches until his death. He was also a Privy Councillor.\n\nDodson edited reports of Admiralty cases and served as a civil lawyer. He died in London on 27 April 1858. His son, John George Dodson, became an MP and was later created Lord Monk Bretton.",
